3.text:
In an air ionisation chamber

* $I=\dot Q$ and  $E_{dep}=\displaystyle\frac{W}{e}\,Q$

so that for the mass $m$ which is actually seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{\rm air}= \frac{\dot E_{dep}}{m}= 
\frac{I}{m}\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

is the air kerma rate in the beam.  For a beam which is thin in air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{dep}= \Phi E\;\mu_{en}\rho V_{i} ,
\qquad V_{i}=A_{f}\,L ,
\end{equation*}

$A_{f}$ is the beam area at the focus, $L$ the length of the chamber
in the beam direction,  $\Phi$ the photon fluence rate and
$E=33.0\,{\rm keV}$.  With  $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{\rm air}= \frac{I}{m}\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

is the kerma rate which the tissue sees, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$.

$A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{i}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m=\rho_{a}V_{i}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
0.2718\,{\rm cm^{3}}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}.
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{\rm air}= \frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\ {\rm J\,C^{-1}}}
{3.52\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
\approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous air kerma rate in the
$0.3\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  In photon fluence terms

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot D}{E\mu_{en}}\approx
\frac{1.9\times10^{-4}}{5.29\times10^{-15}\ {\rm J}\times0.0424\ {\rm
m^{-1}}}\approx 8\!-\!9\times10^{11}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

about $8\times10^{7}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at 33 keV, the usual
synchrotron imaging value.

For a point on the surface the beam is present for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
      =\Big[\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x}\Big]\,\Delta t ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. the effective exposure time of a surface element.  With the
quoted

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\simeq 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the kerma actually accumulated by one surface element per energy
step is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot D_{\rm air}\;t_{d}
      \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \approx 3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, about $3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm rad}$
per 33 keV exposure step.  This is the instantaneous $\approx
0.2\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma rate $\, \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
at the focus reduced to the $\,t_{d}\simeq20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell of a
point on the scanned surface.  The air kerma is essentially the tissue
kerma because $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\ {\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV.  For $N_{E}$ energy steps the surface dose is
$N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g. $\approx 8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for two energies and
$\approx12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for three energies, i.e. a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
multiple-energy scan in the usual synchrotron micro-imaging regime.
This is the $\sim10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}\,$Gy surface kerma normally quoted
for the 33 keV bent-Laue multiple-energy system.

2.text:
For the air filled chamber the charge which is created in the gas is

* $I=\dot N_{i} e $ ,      $\dot N_{i}$ the number of ion pairs made per s
* $\dot E_{\rm air}= \dot N_{i}W= I\,W/e $

with $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air.  With the 2.0 pA which is read
on the chamber

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm air}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times 10^{-12}\times 33.97
               \simeq 6.8\times 10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the air which is actually illuminated by the beam.  The air which is
illuminated at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
V_{\rm ill}=A_{f}L_{c}
\end{equation*}

with $A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\,{\rm
cm^{2}}$ the focal spot and $L_{c}=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ the length of the
chamber along the beam.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
V_{\rm ill}=0.018\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{\rm air}= \rho_{\rm air}V_{\rm ill}
           =1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
           \simeq 3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

The air kerma rate in the focal spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{\rm air}= \frac{\dot E_{\rm air}}{m_{\rm air}}
      =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{\rm air}}
      \simeq\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
      \approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air at the focus.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm air}$ the
kerma in soft tissue is the same to a very good approximation and

\begin{equation*}
\dot D\simeq\dot K_{\rm air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx
0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
is the instantaneous dose rate which a piece of tissue would see while it
is actually in the $0.3\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ beam.

A point of the object is in the beam for the time it takes the scan to
move the beam width past the point,

\begin{equation*}
t_{b}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{e}} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the numbers quoted

\begin{equation*}
t_{b}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{e}}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} .
\end{equation*}
Hence the dose actually accumulated by a given surface element in one
single-energy projection is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D\,t_{b}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
per energy, i.e. $\approx 3$–$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$
per single-energy pass, essentially the air kerma received while the
$0.3\,{\rm mm}$ beam dwells on the point.  In the usual dual-energy
implementation this is $2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ to the point,
$\approx12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy set etc., which is the
$O(10^{-6}{\rm \,Gy})$ cumulative surface dose quoted for synchrotron
multi-energy micro-imaging.  For a single 33 keV projection the quoted
$\,\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ is often given as $ \approx 0.004\,{\rm mGy}$
to the surface, essentially the instantaneous $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
kerma rate integrated over the $\approx$20 ms beam dwell.  This is the
conventionally quoted $\,{\rm \sim\mu Gy}$ per projection level; multiplying
by the number of energies used in the actual multi-energy set gives the
total cumulative surface kerma.

