1.text:
In an air ionisation chamber the power which is deposited in the air
which is actually in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= I\,\frac{W}{e}\;, \qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

so that with $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is illuminated at any instant.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{i}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{i}
       =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
       \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

and the air kerma rate in the $0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ line focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
           \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
           \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. the instantaneous kerma rate seen by a piece of tissue which is
standing in the focus.  With

\begin{equation*}
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}\simeq
\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{air}=0.328\ {\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at 33 keV the same numerical value is the absorbed dose rate in soft
tissue in charged particle equilibrium,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the focus.

A surface element is in the horizontal $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\Bigl[\frac{w_{f}}{\rm step}\Bigr]t_{exp}
     \approx2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted for the raster.  The dose accumulated by that element
on one passage of the line focus, i.e. per energy step, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV projection to the surface in charged particle equilibrium,
$\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\,{\rm mGy}$ per projection.  This is the
conventionally quoted $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per micro-beam projection for
the bent-Laue system.

With $N_{E}$ energies the point dose is $D_{N_{E}}=N_{E}D_{1}$.  For the
usual dual-energy K-edge subtraction, $N_{E}=2$,

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\approx2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

to a surface element which is traversed once by the line focus, $\approx
12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three energy acquisition.  The $ \dot D_{t}\approx
0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ is the instantaneous tissue dose rate in the
$0.3\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus and $D_{1}\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ is the
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell dose per energy, essentially the $\sim10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}$
surface kerma normally quoted for synchrotron multiple-energy micro
imaging.  This is $D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx0.004\,{\rm mGy}$
per single 33 keV exposure, the $\sim8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ dual-energy value
being the cumulative dose normally quoted for the system.

